title The key that cost more than the compile
prompt take the Bakery baton -- core infrastructure, Lindy-first and crux-first
think Fire's rota says read the ledger before reading anything new, so the open reds came first; %646's class already carries an instrument (precondition_dependent), and %689's doors wait on Keaton.
think My own lane's item 2 is content-keyed compilation, so I priced the receipt rather than assuming it saved what it claimed.
think A receipt HIT on a one-file program cost 1,492 ms; hashing the pinned 172 MB toolchain alone cost 1,420 ms of it, and reading those same bytes without hashing cost 124 ms -- so the cost is SHA-256 in software at about 120 MB/s.
think Two cures were tested and dropped before the third: -mcpu=native moved the reading by nothing, and this standard library's BLAKE3 measured 3,937 ms, three times slower again.
obs The repair has two halves -- decide before you read (wants_key, from bytes already in hand) and remember the compiler's digest per file under inode, size, mtime and ctime.
obs ctime is the reading that earns the design: it catches a same-size rewrite in place whose modification time was restored, which the other three miss.
obs Three mutations were planted and each bit: dropping ctime reds leg 21, never consulting the record reds leg 19, and skipping the path comparison reds leg 20 -- the last only after leg 20 was sharpened, since its first spelling passed either way.
obs The cold roster was stopped twice by my own hand -- once to land the claim the baton asks for before a build, once because eight ships' contention put a full pass past three hours. It read 44 guards, 0 red, before it stopped; the hot scoped pass is this lap's standing proof.
